<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-12">
    <title>Week 12</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            <alert>Reading covers chapter 3 and L</alert>
        </p>
        <p>
            <alert>Reading Quiz 16 Covers 3.1 through 3.6</alert>
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-chap3">
        <title>Spacetime Physics Chapter 3</title>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Section 3.1 describes the principle of relativity formally. This
                        is already present in mechanics, and Einstein's realization is that
                        it should be extended to all laws of physics
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Section 3.2 reminds us that many things are not the same in different
                        inertial reference frames. Even in Newtonian mechanics something like
                        the kinetic energy depends on your choice of reference frame, but the
                        <em>work-energy theorem</em> is valid in every frame. Special relativity
                        will take some getting used to, but in a sense it's just slightly
                        extending the list of things that aren't the same.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Section 3.3 tells us about a few things that should be the same in
                        every reference frame, and sections 3.4 through 3.6 begin exploring
                        the consequences of the invariance of the speed of light. Loss of
                        simultaneity turns out to be the most difficult concept to get used to.
                        As you'll see in your study, most of the paradoxes that you find in your
                        head usually involve forgetting about the relativity of simultaneity.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        We will cover the quantitative form of the effects, so for now the
                        thing to pay attention is how carefully the textbook crafts its
                        arguments. Since our intuition is not prepared for some of the
                        consequences of special relativity, we must be very thorough when
                        using the postulates to analyze a physical situation.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Sections 3.7 and 3.8 then establish the invariance of the interval,
                        which the textbook uses as a guiding principle to understand special
                        relativity. The mathematical form of the interval is strange with its
                        minus sign, but it does allow for a very geometric way of visualizing
                        relativity.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-chapL">
        <title>Spacetime Physics Chapter L</title>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Chapter L is focused on deriving the <term>Lorentz transformations</term>,
                        which tell us how to relate measurements made in two reference frames. The
                        textbook correctly points out that the geometric view of relativity is more
                        fundamental, but when you're trying to solve a physics problem, you're
                        usually using a coordinate system, so understanding how to relate coordinates
                        is useful and worthwhile.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        We'll go through the derivation in a slightly different way than the text,
                        but the key ingredients are all there: the transformation is linear and
                        keeps the interval invariant. Having the transformations allows you to be
                        very systematic in problem solving. Once you set up a list of <term>events</term>
                        and their coordinates in a particular frame, the Lorentz transformations
                        tell you the coordinates in any other frame, so you can use this to make
                        sense of another observer's measurements.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </subsection>
</section>